From cc87202d3f3c3c6b5ce1ff1deaffbfaa778e97ef Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ian Blenke <ian@blenke.com>
Date: Wed, 15 Jul 2026 01:22:34 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 07178/11362] [outer-loop] fix local submission gate's own
 thread self-oversubscription

Third fix in the same submission-prep pre-flight session, after
REQ-ARC-FCP-5591-3 (620bf5f65) and REQ-CAPSTONE-4556-2 (c15dcb972).
Both of those were verified fast in isolation, but the gate itself
still failed 3 consecutive times with IDENTICAL results (1/8 solved,
7777 actions, 7 timed_out) regardless of how quiet the system looked
right before each launch -- inconsistent with a code regression, since
isolated per-game runs were fast and clean every time.

Root cause: _measure_game() runs 8x concurrently via measure()'s
ThreadPoolExecutor(max_workers=8), each spawning a fresh
arc_leaderboard_eval.py subprocess. Verified directly via
/proc/PID/status: a single such subprocess spawns 24 threads on this
24-core box (numpy/scipy/torch default to one OpenMP/OpenBLAS thread
per core with no thread-count env var set). 8 unconstrained subprocesses
in parallel means up to 192 threads contending for 24 cores -- severe,
fully self-inflicted oversubscription, independent of whatever the
concurrently-running research conductor happened to be doing.

Fixed by pinning OMP_NUM_THREADS/OPENBLAS_NUM_THREADS/MKL_NUM_THREADS/
NUMEXPR_NUM_THREADS/VECLIB_MAXIMUM_THREADS to 1 in each subprocess's
env -- verified directly (single subprocess drops from 24 threads to
1). Each game-eval subprocess does small-grid, Python-level search
work and doesn't benefit from multi-threaded BLAS at this scale, so
pinning to 1 thread each lets all 8 run truly in parallel within the
24-core budget instead of starving each other.

Verified end-to-end: a gate run immediately after this fix PASSED --
7/8 solved (up from the 4/8 baseline), 0 timed out, "PASS (IMPROVED):
CORE per-level efficiency 2.412 vs baseline 2.0074", 3 bonus solves
(cd82, ft09, su15). This closes out the submission-prep pre-flight:
all three issues (two real algorithmic bugs plus the gate's own
measurement artifact) are now resolved and independently verified.
